#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199522 :tutorial/content "The **negation** of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lnot P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true if and only if $P$ is false for a specific set of assignment variables $x_1,x_2, \\ldots x_n.$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb Z.$\n\n$\\qquad$ *$P(m):$ $m$ is even*\n\nThen, $\\lnot P(m)$ is a new predicate that reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is **not** even*\n\nThis can be simplified as:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is odd*\n\nFor example:\n\n- $P(2)$ is true while $\\lnot P(2)$ is false\n\n- $P(3)$ is false while $\\lnot P(3)$ is true" :tutorial/id #uuid "282e591f-3d63-58ed-8e4e-852ba57975cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7988 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199543 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ be the truth set of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n).$\n\nFrom the definition of the negation of a predicate, we have that $\\lnot P$ is true if and only if $P$ is false. Therefore, the truth set of $\\neg P$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{\\neg P} = \\overline{T}_P\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n$P(x):$ $-2x +3 \\leq 5$\n\nTo find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & \\le 5 \\\\ -2x & \\le 2 \\\\ x & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = [-1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{[-1,\\infty )} \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3dd43712-0f20-5210-9cba-7ef53f91ef83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8141 :tutorial/title "Truth Sets of Negations"}]}
